sstream and stringstream in C++
The <sstream> header in C++ provides a way to handle string-based input and output operations, similar to how we use <iostream> for standard input/output. It includes three main classes:

std::stringstream: Used for both input and output operations on strings.
std::istringstream: Used only for input operations on strings.
std::ostringstream: Used only for output operations on strings.
Among these, std::stringstream is the most versatile because it allows both input and output on strings.

1. What is std::stringstream?
std::stringstream is a stream class that allows us to manipulate strings as if they were input/output streams. It enables:

String parsing (extracting data from a string)
String formatting (constructing a formatted string)
Conversion between different data types

2. How to use std::stringstream?
You need to include <sstream> to use it.

8. When to Use stringstream?
Parsing structured data: When working with space-separated or comma-separated values.
Type conversion: Easily convert between int, float, double, and string.
Building formatted strings: Instead of using multiple + operators, use stringstream to append values efficiently.

9. Conclusion
<sstream> provides powerful tools to work with string-based I/O.
std::stringstream allows both input and output operations.
std::istringstream is for input only, while std::ostringstream is for output only.
It is useful for parsing, formatting, and converting between types.